[center]
[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]NCZoningCore[/b][/size][/color]
[i]The NC Zoning registry, in game, for other mods to build on.[/i][/center]
[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]What is this?[/b][/size][/color]
[b]NCZoningCore[/b] is a framework (a modder's resource). It fetches the [b]NC Zoning[/b] registry, an interactive map of Cyberpunk 2077 location mods, and exposes it in game as a read-only API that other mods can use: each mapped location's name, authors, world coordinates, category, tags, and district.

[b]It has no menu and does nothing on its own.[/b] You only need it if another mod lists it as a requirement. Installing it by itself has no visible effect.
[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Part of the NC Zoning project[/b][/size][/color]
NCZoningCore is the in-game half of [b]NC Zoning[/b], the interactive map of Cyberpunk 2077 location mods.
[list]
[*][b]Explore the map:[/b] [url=https://nczoning.net]nczoning.net[/url]
[*][b]Join the community:[/b] [url=https://discord.gg/sc4yEx2fNf]Locations Hub Discord[/url]
[/list]
[center][b]Showcase[/b]
[youtube]30Daism2E-c[/youtube]
[b]Trailer[/b]
[youtube]LJVJ2lN4aQ0[/youtube][/center]
[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Do I need to install this?[/b][/size][/color]
Only if a mod you want tells you to. NCZoningCore is a shared dependency, like Codeware or RED4ext: it sits in the background and provides data to the mods that ask for it.

[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]What it does under the hood[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*]Downloads the registry once per game session over HTTPS.
[*]Caches it on disk, so it works offline after the first online launch and revalidates efficiently (no re-download when nothing changed).
[*]Serves the data to redscript and CET Lua mods through a small, stable API.
[/list]
It makes [b]no[/b] changes to your game: no writes, no telemetry, no position tracking, no UI.
[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*]RED4ext
[*]redscript
[*]Codeware
[*]RedData
[*]RedFileSystem
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/31920]RedLogger[/url]
[/list]
[i]RedLogger is what NCZoningCore writes its log to. It gives this mod its own file at [font=Courier New]r6\logs\mods\NCZoningCore__<date_time>.log[/font] instead of shouting into a log shared with every other mod, and it keeps the five most recent sessions. If something goes wrong, that one small file is the whole answer — which matters more here than in most mods, because NCZoningCore runs in the background and the thing that goes wrong is usually "the registry never loaded".[/i]
[b]Optional:[/b]
[list]
[*]RedHttpClient
[/list]
[i]Cyber Engine Tweaks is [b]not[/b] required by NCZoningCore itself. It is only needed if the consumer mod using NCZoningCore is a CET Lua mod.[/i]
[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]RedHttpClient is optional[/b][/size][/color]
RedHttpClient is the plugin that lets the game make HTTPS requests, and it is the only reason NCZoningCore touches the network at all. If you would rather not install a general-purpose HTTP plugin, that is a fair call, and you do not have to. It is a [b]soft dependency[/b]: NCZoningCore installs and runs without it.

[b]With RedHttpClient.[/b] The registry downloads itself once per session and keeps itself current. Nothing for you to do.

[b]Without RedHttpClient.[/b] NCZoningCore never opens a socket. It reads the registry from a file you download yourself, and treats it as permanently out of date, because it has no way to check. You re-download whenever you want fresher data.

If you skip RedHttpClient and do not supply the file, the mod has no data. It will tell you so on screen a few seconds into your session, rather than pretending the map is empty.

[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Getting the data by hand[/b][/size][/color]
Open a terminal in your Cyberpunk 2077 folder and run:
[code]curl.exe -L "https://api.nczoning.net/v1/locations?full=1" --create-dirs -o "r6\storages\NCZoningCore\locations_full.json"[/code]
That creates [font=Courier New]Cyberpunk 2077\r6\storages\NCZoningCore\locations_full.json[/font]. The folder is not negotiable: RedFileSystem confines every mod to [font=Courier New]r6\storages\<mod name>\[/font], and NCZoningCore cannot read from anywhere else. There is only one file to fetch.

The output path is relative, so check it landed where you meant. Downloading a perfectly good file into the wrong folder looks like a complete success and does nothing:
[code]dir r6\storages\NCZoningCore[/code]
You should see [font=Courier New]locations_full.json[/font], around 210 KB.

[b]Vortex, or a manual install:[/b] nothing special to do. The command above is all you need.

[b]Mod Organizer 2:[/b] the game directory is virtualised, so instead either run the command from your [font=Courier New]overwrite\[/font] folder, or create [font=Courier New]r6\storages\NCZoningCore\locations_full.json[/font] inside a mod folder and let MO2 deploy it (which keeps it out of overwrite and lets you uninstall it cleanly).

The overwrite route is the same place the mod writes to anyway: with RedHttpClient installed, the game's write passes through the virtual filesystem and MO2 drops it in [font=Courier New]overwrite\r6\storages\NCZoningCore\[/font]. So if you add RedHttpClient later, it refreshes the file you already put there instead of creating a second copy elsewhere.
[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size][/color]
[list=1]
[*]Install the requirements above.
[*]Download NCZoningCore and extract the [font=Courier New]r6[/font] folder into your Cyberpunk 2077 root directory.
[i]Final path should look like:[/i] [font=Courier New]Cyberpunk 2077\r6\scripts\NCZoningCore\[/font]
[*][i]Only if you skipped RedHttpClient:[/i] run the [font=Courier New]curl[/font] command above to download the registry.
[/list]
Works with mod managers, or install manually.
[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]For mod authors[/b][/size][/color]
Build on NCZoningCore from redscript or CET Lua. The full consumer guide and worked examples for both are in the GitHub repository linked on this page.

In short: redscript consumers [font=Courier New]import NCZoning.Api.*[/font] (or guard with [font=Courier New]@if(ModuleExists("NCZoning.Api"))[/font] for a soft dependency) and subscribe to the [font=Courier New]NCZoning-DataReady[/font] / [font=Courier New]-DataRefreshed[/font] / [font=Courier New]-DataError[/font] events. CET Lua consumers call the [font=Courier New]NCZoningApi[/font] facade and observe [font=Courier New]NCZoningApi.OnDataReady[/font] for the data-ready signal.
[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]A note on game updates[/b][/size][/color]
NCZoningCore is pure redscript and survives game patches on its own, but its plugin requirements (Codeware and the Red* plugins) usually need a rebuild by their authors after each Cyberpunk update. Right after a game update the framework can be temporarily unavailable until those catch up. Mods that depend on it with a soft dependency keep working in that window.
